<% content_for :title, "Terms" %>

<div class="l-container">
  <h1><%= multicolor("Terms") %></h1>

  <p class="m-lede">
    TL;DR: You keep full ownership of every puzzle you make here. We take the bare minimum permission needed to run the site, and not a drop more.
  </p>

  <hr />

  <h2>Your quartets are yours</h2>
  <p>
    You own the copyright in every quartet you create — all the words and categories and the whole thing. 
    Saving/publishing here transfers
    nothing nothing to us save for the right to host it (see below). 
    We don't own your puzzles, we can't sell your puzzles, and we don't
    relicense them to anyone. This is true whether you have an account or made
    your quartet anonymously.
  </p>

  <h2>The one permission you give us</h2>
  <p>
    By saving a quartet on Quartets you grant us a <strong>non-exclusive,
    royalty-free license</strong> to host, store, display, and share it. Sorta need that 
  bit to actually have a site with puzzles on it. Otherwise, like, we'd be legally barred 
  from letting you create puzzles at all, let alone saving them and showing them to people. 
  So, that's the entire grant.</p>
  <p>THAT SAID... there's one bit of complexity: deletion. So, if you create a puzzle and no one 
  ever plays it and so there's zero stats attached to it, when you hit delete it just deletes. And
  our <strong>non-exclusive royalty-free license</strong> ends there. NBD.
  BUT. If a puzzle has some stats attached to it cause some people solved it, we just "soft-delete" it
  where it effectively removes any and all access to the puzzle. The puzzle data itself stats in the db
  cause otherwise players would lose their previous play stats. Not even the admins can pull it up on the site —
  we'd have to do a super annoying and tedious thing where we actually dig through the db to find it and no way 
  we're doing that just to view one puzzle. So we give up the part of the license about displaying
  and sharing it, but not hosting and storing it. 
  </p>

  <h2>What everyone else gets</h2>
  <p>
    They get to play it. And that's the extent of it. Publishing a quartet does <em>not</em> put
    it under an open license. Nobody may copy, republish, or sell your puzzle
    without your say-so (which you'd just, y'know, give them. Elsewhere we don't have any sort of 
    mechanism for doing that on the site (yet)). So if you <em>want</em> your work to be remixable, that's
    your right to give away, wherever and however you like. We might add a checkbox or something
    to puzzle creation that lets people choose an open-license, but I dunno. I'm debating that. 
    Too much cruft on the form that most people don't give a shit about can be overwhelming.
    Maybe a user setting in the profile...
  </p>

  <h2>Our own content</h2>
  <p>
    The site's code is <%= link_to "MIT", "https://github.com/johnhutch/quartets/blob/main/LICENSE", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>;
    the content <em>we</em> wrote (and by "we" I pretty just mean Hutch. Who is me. So, my puzzles, the site copy, the docs, all that stuff)
    is <%= link_to "CC BY 4.0", "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>.
    Those licenses cover our (my) work only, never yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Ground rules</h2>
  <p>
    Only publish what's yours to publish. Don't be a dick and post someone else's puzzle. That's lame AF. 
    Also, don't post hate-garbage or spam. Our moderators can (and will) unpublish or remove content that
    breaks these rules. It's my site and the mods and I will kick that MAGA bullshit right the fuck off the site, IDGAF. 
    But yeah, other than that, that's the whole extent of our editorial power. And even with all that in mind, 
    us removing a puzzle, no matter how vile, never touches your ownership of it. So. Enjoy posting that on some 
    MAGA-chud, right-wing-snowflake, echo-chamber garbage site, I guess. And also go fuck yourself. That's in our legal terms
    and conditions, so you're legally required to do it.
  </p>

  <h2>If someone stole your work</h2>
  <p>
    If a quartet on this site lifts your work (or someone lifted yours) tell
    us at <%= link_to "johnhutch.com", "https://johnhutch.com", target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" %>
  and we'll try sort it out. Usually that means taking the copy down — maybe even scolding the dork who stole
  your shit. Maybe some kinda public shaming mechanism... Hrm.</p>
  <p>BUT ALSO sometimes it also means defending content creators from bullshit DMCA takedowns. Which we have experience doing. So
  you should probably just skip it and avoid wasting both our time. 
  </p>

  <p><%= link_to "← Back to the quartets", root_path %></p>
</div>
